<article class="env-lemma" id="0F12">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F12">Lemma <span data-tag="0F12">59.96.5</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a quasi-compact and quasi-separated scheme such that $\text{cd}(X) < \infty $. Let $\Lambda $ be a torsion ring. Let $E \in D(X_{\acute{e}tale}, \Lambda )$ and $K \in D(\Lambda )$. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R\Gamma (X, E \otimes _\Lambda ^\mathbf {L} \underline{K}) = R\Gamma (X, E) \otimes _\Lambda ^\mathbf {L} K  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      There is a canonical map from right to left by Cohomology on Sites, Section <a href="/tag/0943" data-tag="0943">21.50</a>. Let $T(K)$ be the property that the statement of the lemma holds for $K \in D(\Lambda )$. We will check conditions (1), (2), and (3) of More on Algebra, Remark <a href="/tag/09PB" data-tag="09PB">15.60.11</a> hold for $T$ to conclude. Property (1) holds because both sides of the equality commute with direct sums, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0F11" data-tag="0F11">59.96.3</a>. Property (2) holds because we are comparing exact functors between triangulated categories and we can use Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/014A" data-tag="014A">13.4.3</a>. Property (3) says the lemma holds when $K = \Lambda [k]$ for any shift $k \in \mathbf{Z}$ and this is obvious. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>